// ATTACK (red team, concurrency lens round 3): does an uncaught `throw`
// inside a `coop_spawn`ed cooperative task get treated with the SAME
// fatal severity an uncaught throw inside a `spawn`ed OS thread now has
// (LEDGER item 16, docs/09-concurrency.md "Error handling in spawned
// blocks"), or does it silently vanish and leave a consumer hanging
// forever -- the exact bug class that was deliberately fixed for `spawn`?
//
// kryos-rt/src/spawn.rs's OS-thread entry point calls
// `kryos_exception_report_thread_FATAL_if_pending()` (process::exit(101))
// on an uncaught throw.
//
// kryos-rt/src/executor.rs's `kryos_coop_spawn` task-thread closure
// (line ~148) instead calls the NON-FATAL
// `kryos_exception_report_thread_if_pending()` -- report-only, task marked
// done, baton handed back, PROCESS CONTINUES. Its own comment claims parity
// ("report it like the spawn runtime does") but calls the report-only
// twin, not the fatal one spawn.rs actually uses.
//
// Because a Kryos throw is a thread-local flag + synthesized early return
// (no real unwind), every statement AFTER the throw point in the task body
// is silently skipped -- including, here, the `send(ch, idx)` that a
// consumer is blocked waiting for. If the swallow is real, `recv(ch)`
// below for the second value hangs forever instead of the process dying
// loudly with exit 101 the way an equivalent `spawn` throw would.
fn worker(ch: i64, idx: i64) {
    if idx == 1 {
        throw "boom from coop task 1"
    }
    send(ch, idx)
}

fn main() {
    let ch = chan()
    coop_spawn(worker(ch, 0))
    coop_spawn(worker(ch, 1))
    coop_run()
    println("coop_run returned")
    let a = recv(ch)
    println("got a=" + to_string(a))
    println("about to recv second value -- if the coop throw was silently swallowed instead of fatal, this HANGS FOREVER")
    let b = recv(ch)
    println("got b=" + to_string(b))
}
